\subsection{Files and keys}{

The export is a set of tables joined by id, one Scholastica submission
version per manuscript row.
\itemize{
\item \code{manuscripts.csv} has one row per version. \code{manuscript_id} is unique to
that version. \code{previous_version_id} is the previous version's
\code{manuscript_id} (empty on the first version). \code{submission_id} is shared by
every version of the same paper, so it identifies the paper, but it is not
reliably the first or the latest \code{manuscript_id}. \code{version_number} is 1,
2, or 3. \code{created_at} is when that version hit the desk. \code{workflow_state}
is the current state of that version. \code{decision_created_at} is when the
decision on that version was made.
\item \code{decisions.csv} has one row per decided version, joined on \code{manuscript_id}.
\code{decision_type} is \code{accept}, \code{reject}, or \code{revise_and_resubmit}. Its
\code{created_at} matches \code{manuscripts.csv} \code{decision_created_at}.
\item \code{reviewer-invitations.csv} is joined on the version \code{manuscript_id}.
\code{created_at} is when the invitation was sent. \code{accepted_at} is when the
reviewer accepted. \code{workflow_state} is \code{submitted} (review filed),
\code{accepted} (agreed, review not in), \code{declined}, \code{expired}, \code{revoked}, or
\code{hard_bounced}. \code{manuscript_version_number} repeats the version.
\item \code{reviews.csv} has no \code{manuscript_id}. It joins to an invitation through
\code{reviewer_invitation_id}. \code{created_at} is when the review was filed.
\code{publication_recommendation} is Accept, Revise and resubmit, or Reject.
\item \code{authors.csv} joins on \code{manuscript_id}. \code{articles.csv} is the published
article, joined on \code{manuscript_id}, with \code{published_at}. \code{activities.csv}
is a prose log of the same events (submitted, invited, accepted an
invitation, submitted a review, made a decision). The clocks below use the
structured timestamps, not the prose log. \code{issues.csv} is journal issues.
\code{posts.csv} is empty in the export this function was written against.
}

Trial rows are dropped: \code{title} beginning with \verb{[EXAMPLE]}, or \code{editor_tags}
containing \verb{trial entries to ignore}.

A paper is the chain walked from a version with no \code{previous_version_id}
along \code{previous_version_id}. Outcomes come from the latest version's
\code{workflow_state}: \code{ready_to_publish} accepted, \code{rejected} or \code{desk_rejected}
rejected, \code{revise_and_resubmit} with no later version still with the author,
\code{reviewers_confirmed} or \code{reviews_submitted} still in review, and
\code{withdrawn_before_decision} or \code{withdrawn_after_non_terminal_decision}
withdrawn.
}

\subsection{Seconds columns already on manuscripts.csv}{

Scholastica also stores durations. They are not what the report plots,
because the total mixes the two clocks, and the editor buckets sometimes
overlap.
\itemize{
\item \code{time_to_decision_in_seconds} is journal time on that version only
(\code{created_at} to \code{decision_created_at}). It does not include the author gap.
\item \code{time_spent_with_editor_pre_review_in_seconds} is meant to be desk time
before review. On many versions it matches the wait until the first
reviewer accepts. On others it keeps running after reviews have arrived.
\item \code{time_spent_with_editor_post_review_in_seconds} matches last review filed
to the decision.
\item On two versions, pre-review plus post-review exceeds time-to-decision, so
those two buckets are not a partition.
\item \code{time_spent_with_author_in_seconds} is stored on the new version and
matches the gap from the previous decision to this version's \code{created_at}.
\item \code{total_time_to_decision_across_versions_in_seconds} sums version
time-to-decision and the author gaps. It is filled on the latest decided
version. A paper reviewed in two days, held by the author for a year, and
then accepted the next day has a total near a year.
}
}

\subsection{Clocks the report uses}{

Timestamps are minute resolution (\verb{2015-01-13 10:18PM} style). Durations are
in days.
\itemize{
\item Hits the desk: that version's \code{created_at}.
\item Goes out to a reviewer: invitation \code{created_at}.
\item Reviewer takes it on: invitation \code{accepted_at}.
\item Review comes back: \code{reviews.csv} \code{created_at}.
\item Decision: \code{decision_created_at}.
\item Triage, until a reviewer has it: version \code{created_at} to the first
\code{accepted_at}. Time until the first invitation is sent is reported beside it.
\item Review window: first \code{accepted_at} to the last review filed on that version.
\item Editor after the reviews are in: last review to the decision.
\item Round-1 journal time: first version \code{created_at} to its decision. Desk
rejects and editorial accepts with no invitation are in this total, and
are also reported on their own.
\item Author revision time: decision on version N to \code{created_at} of version N+1.
Each return is one observation. An open revise-and-resubmit is aged to
\code{asOf} and kept out of the completed mean.
\item Time to accept a revision: \code{created_at} to the accept decision on a version
whose \code{decision_type} is \code{accept} and whose \code{version_number} is greater
than 1.
\item Journal time for a finished paper: sum over its versions of
(decision − arrived). Author gaps are not in that sum.
\item Calendar time: first version \code{created_at} to the final decision. This is
journal time plus author time.
}

Versions that have not yet been decided are left out of the completed
journal means. Their elapsed time is listed under papers still open.
}
}
